@page "/testing"

<DocPage Href="testing" Title="Testing"
         Lead="Stores are plain objects, so they test like plain objects - and Blex.Testing adds a harness for asserting on the dispatch pipeline itself.">

    <h2>The simplest test</h2>
    <p>
        A store needs no host, no DI container and no Blazor. Construct it, call an action, assert on
        a property.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@Plain" FileName="CounterTests.cs" />

    <p>
        That covers state. What it does not cover is whether the <em>right action</em> was dispatched,
        with the right payload, exactly once. That is what the harness is for.
    </p>

    <h2>The harness</h2>
    <p>
        <code>TestHarnessBlex&lt;TStore&gt;</code> creates a fresh <code>ManagerBlex</code>,
        registers your store with it, and starts recording every action.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@Harness" />
    <ApiTable Title="TestHarnessBlex&lt;TStore&gt;" Rows="@HarnessRows" />

    <h2>The action log</h2>
    <p>
        <code>ActionLogBlex</code> records each dispatch with its name, sequence, arguments and the global
        state immediately afterwards.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@Log" />
    <ApiTable Title="ActionLogBlex" Rows="@LogRows" />
    <ApiTable Title="RecordedActionBlex" Rows="@RecordedRows" />

    <h2>Asserting on payloads</h2>
    <p>Action arguments are captured automatically, so you can assert on exactly what was dispatched.</p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@Payload" />

    <h2>Testing effects</h2>
    <p>
        Awaiting the effect wrapper is usually enough. When you need to observe an intermediate state
        - a loading flag, say - <code>WaitForAsync</code> beats sprinkling <code>Task.Delay</code>
        through the test: it re-evaluates the condition on every store change and throws
        <code>TimeoutException</code> if it never becomes true.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@Effects" />

    <h3>Errors and cancellation</h3>
    <CodeBlock Code="@EffectErrors" />

    <h2>Asserting on batching</h2>
    <p>
        <code>CountNotifications</code> counts how many times a store raised
        <code>StateChanged</code> while an action ran - the direct way to prove that a multi-field
        action really does render once.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@Notifications" />

    <h2>Testing middleware</h2>
    <p>
        A store is rarely alone in production - a veto filter, an audit logger or a metrics recorder
        usually wraps it, and those guard rails deserve tests too.
        <code>TestHarnessBlex.WithFilter</code> and <code>WithMiddleware</code> give you the same
        zero-setup harness with the pipeline attached.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@MiddlewareTest" />

    <p>
        Reach for a hand-built <code>ManagerBlex</code> only when the test genuinely needs more than
        one store, or a manager configured before any store is registered.
    </p>

    <h2>Testing persistence</h2>
    <p>
        <code>IStorageBlex</code> is a three-method interface, so an in-memory fake is trivial.
        Remember to <code>FlushAsync</code> when the persistor is debounced.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@PersistenceTest" />

    <h2>Testing undo/redo</h2>
    <CodeBlock Code="@HistoryTest" />

    <h2>Extension methods</h2>
    <ApiTable Title="TestExtensionsBlex" Rows="@ExtensionRows" />

    <Callout Kind="Callout.CalloutKind.Tip" Title="Snapshots make good assertions">
        <p style="margin-bottom:0">
            <code>harness.Snapshot()</code> and <code>harness.State</code> return the same JSON the
            library uses for persistence and time-travel. Asserting against a snapshot checks the
            whole store at once, and it fails loudly when a field you forgot about changes.
        </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout Kind="Callout.CalloutKind.Gotcha" Title="Dispose the harness">
        <p style="margin-bottom:0">
            The harness holds a live subscription on its manager. Use <code>using var</code> - or
            <code>Dispose()</code> - so a long test class does not accumulate recorders. The
            harness's manager is its own, so tests never interfere with each other.
        </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2>Testing components</h2>
    <p>
        For component-level tests with bUnit, register the store the same way the app does and let
        the component subscribe. There is nothing Blex-specific to mock.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@Bunit" />

</DocPage>

@code {
